{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789094198115,"sessionID":"ses_f72344e8bffef224SU4Jqwq9zP","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b97b074cc8a10ef0","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Volumes/SSD990/llm/tmp/opencode/ecdlp-20260910/coordination/experiments/EXP-ECDLP-c373eb/batches/BATCH-e8a3a7/reviews/TASK-20260903-d02517/red-team-report.yaml","limit":80,"offset":120},"output":"<path>/Volumes/SSD990/llm/tmp/opencode/ecdlp-20260910/coordination/experiments/EXP-ECDLP-c373eb/batches/BATCH-e8a3a7/reviews/TASK-20260903-d02517/red-team-report.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n120:     - id: OBJ-1\n121:       title: >-\n122:         The two runs are one implementation, not two: 266 of 270 executable\n123:         lines are byte-identical, and every function definition is shared\n124:         verbatim.\n125:       line_of_attack: '(a) common-mode failure'\n126:       severity: decisive\n127:       finding: >-\n128:         `diff` over the two committed scripts, with comments and blank lines\n129:         stripped, gives 270 executable lines in rc1_tabulation.py, 282 in\n130:         rc1_tabulation_run2.py (the 12 extra are a docstring), and 266 lines in\n131:         common. The complete list of executable differences is five lines:\n132:         `p = 1009` -> `p = 1013`; `A, B = 1, 1` -> `A, B = 2, 2`;\n133:         `n = 47` -> `n = 41`; the six-triple `triples = [...]` literal; and one\n134:         ADDED line `assert p % n != 0`. Nothing else changed. Every function --\n135:         fp_add, fp_mul, count_points, find_point_of_order_n, jac_dbl, jac_add,\n136:         scalar_mult, scalar_mult_naive, curve_ok, valuation, affine_x,\n137:         hensel_lift_y, f_of_x0, vp_diff_x, xk -- is byte-identical, as are the\n138:         Hensel root-lifting block, the null-lift block, both tabulation loops,\n139:         the Part-3 block, the spot-check block and the result assembly.\n140:       why_it_bites: >-\n141:         \"Adapted from\" understates the relationship. RUN-2 is RUN-1's script\n142:         with four constants substituted. The evidential question is the\n143:         likelihood ratio L = P(agree | mechanism correct) / P(agree | shared\n144:         systematic error). For two independent implementations L is large. Here\n145:         P(agree | shared systematic error) = 1 by construction, because the\n146:         error would be in code executed identically in both runs, so L = 1 and\n147:         the agreement carries ZERO weight against the common-mode hypothesis.\n148:         It carries real weight only against errors that are instance-specific\n149:         (a typo in a hard-coded curve constant, an n that is not prime, a bad\n150:         point), and those are the errors the runs' own asserts already catch\n151:         individually.\n152:       what_would_change_it: FG-1, FG-2.\n153: \n154:     - id: OBJ-2\n155:       title: >-\n156:         The order-n certificate -- the single load-bearing check in both runs --\n157:         is computed by the very arithmetic it certifies, and the manifest names\n158:         the block `independent_certificate` while its own prose says otherwise.\n159:       line_of_attack: '(a) common-mode failure'\n160:       severity: high\n161:       finding: >-\n162:         Both scripts establish the lift's correctness with\n163:         `nS_hat = scalar_mult(n, S_hat, MOD); canonical_lift_verified_exact =\n164:         (nS_hat == (0,1,0))`. `scalar_mult` is built from `jac_add`/`jac_dbl`,\n165:         the same routines used for every tabulated value. The supporting asserts\n166:         do not close the loop either: `curve_ok(S_hat, MOD)` tests only that the\n167:         lifted point satisfies the curve equation (membership, not the group\n168:         law); `assert valuation(f0, r, WORK_MOD) == 1` is itself evaluated\n169:         through `f_of_x0` -> `scalar_mult`. RUN-2's manifest records the field\n170:         `canonical_lift_S_hat.independent_certificate` whose own text reads\n171:         \"recomputed via the SAME double-and-add scalar multiplication used\n172:         everywhere else in this script\". The field name asserts an independence\n173:         the field body denies.\n174:       why_it_bites: >-\n175:         My Objection OBJ-6 shows the measured image is FORCED once the order-n\n176:         certificate holds. So the entire empirical content of both runs rests on\n177:         that one certificate -- and that certificate has no implementation\n178:         independence in either run, and shares 100% of its code across the two.\n179:         A wrong-but-self-consistent group law would satisfy it in both runs\n180:         identically. AGENTS.md \"Review architecture\" names exactly this failure\n181:         mode: \"recomputing from the producer's own artifacts reproduces a\n182:         wrong-but-self-consistent implementation faithfully, which is exactly\n183:         the failure mode validation cannot see.\"\n184:       unused_control_already_in_the_file: >-\n185:         Each script already contains a genuinely independent second\n186:         implementation of the group law -- the F_p affine routines `fp_add` /\n187:         `fp_mul`, used only during instance setup -- and NEITHER RUN EVER\n188:         CROSS-CHECKS the Z/p^r Jacobian arithmetic against it. Three lines\n189:         asserting `affine_x(scalar_mult(k, S_hat, MOD), MOD) % p ==\n190:         fp_mul(k, S, p, A)[0]` for k = 1..n-1 would exercise the shared core\n191:         against independent code at zero marginal cost. It is not there.\n192:       what_would_change_it: FG-1, FG-2.\n193: \n194:     - id: OBJ-3\n195:       title: >-\n196:         The null-lift control discriminates a shared MATHEMATICAL-PREMISE error\n197:         and a narrow band of input-invariant code errors. It does NOT\n198:         discriminate a shared CODE error, and it supplies no cross-run\n199:         independence at all because it is itself common-mode.\n\n(Showing lines 120-199 of 961. Use offset=200 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"    - id: OBJ-1\n      title: >-\n        The two runs are one implementation, not two: 266 of 270 executable\n        lines are byte-identical, and every function definition is shared\n        verbatim.\n      line_of_attack: '(a) common-mode failure'\n      severity: decisive\n      finding: >-\n        `diff` over the two committed scripts, with comments and blank lines\n        stripped, gives 270 executable lines in rc1_tabulation.py, 282 in\n        rc1_tabulation_run2.py (the 12 extra are a docstring), and 266 lines in\n        common. The complete list of executable differences is five lines:\n        `p = 1009` -> `p = 1013`; `A, B = 1, 1` -> `A, B = 2, 2`;\n        `n = 47` -> `n = 41`; the six-triple `triples = [...]` literal; and one\n        ADDED line `assert p % n != 0`. Nothing else changed. Every function --\n        fp_add, fp_mul, count_points, find_point_of_order_n, jac_dbl, jac_add,\n        scalar_mult, scalar_mult_naive, curve_ok, valuation, affine_x,\n        hensel_lift_y, f_of_x0, vp_diff_x, xk -- is byte-identical, as are the\n        Hensel root-lifting block, the null-lift block, both tabulation loops,\n        the Part-3 block, the spot-check block and the result assembly.","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Volumes/SSD990/llm/tmp/opencode/ecdlp-20260910/coordination/experiments/EXP-ECDLP-c373eb/batches/BATCH-e8a3a7/reviews/TASK-20260903-d02517/red-team-report.yaml","text":"    - id: OBJ-1\n      title: >-\n        The two runs are one implementation, not two: 266 of 270 executable\n        lines are byte-identical, and every function definition is shared\n        verbatim.\n      line_of_attack: '(a) common-mode failure'\n      severity: decisive\n      finding: >-\n        `diff` over the two committed scripts, with comments and blank lines\n        stripped, gives 270 executable lines in rc1_tabulation.py, 282 in\n        rc1_tabulation_run2.py (the 12 extra are a docstring), and 266 lines in\n        common. The complete list of executable differences is five lines:\n        `p = 1009` -> `p = 1013`; `A, B = 1, 1` -> `A, B = 2, 2`;\n        `n = 47` -> `n = 41`; the six-triple `triples = [...]` literal; and one\n        ADDED line `assert p % n != 0`. Nothing else changed. Every function --\n        fp_add, fp_mul, count_points, find_point_of_order_n, jac_dbl, jac_add,\n        scalar_mult, scalar_mult_naive, curve_ok, valuation, affine_x,\n        hensel_lift_y, f_of_x0, vp_diff_x, xk -- is byte-identical, as are the\n        Hensel root-lifting block, the null-lift block, both tabulation loops,\n        the Part-3 block, the spot-check block and the result assembly.\n      why_it_bites: >-\n        \"Adapted from\" understates the relationship. RUN-2 is RUN-1's script\n        with four constants substituted. The evidential question is the\n        likelihood ratio L = P(agree | mechanism correct) / P(agree | shared\n        systematic error). For two independent implementations L is large. Here\n        P(agree | shared systematic error) = 1 by construction, because the\n        error would be in code executed identically in both runs, so L = 1 and\n        the agreement carries ZERO weight against the common-mode hypothesis.\n        It carries real weight only against errors that are instance-specific\n        (a typo in a hard-coded curve constant, an n that is not prime, a bad\n        point), and those are the errors the runs' own asserts already catch\n        individually.\n      what_would_change_it: FG-1, FG-2.\n\n    - id: OBJ-2\n      title: >-\n        The order-n certificate -- the single load-bearing check in both runs --\n        is computed by the very arithmetic it certifies, and the manifest names\n        the block `independent_certificate` while its own prose says otherwise.\n      line_of_attack: '(a) common-mode failure'\n      severity: high\n      finding: >-\n        Both scripts establish the lift's correctness with\n        `nS_hat = scalar_mult(n, S_hat, MOD); canonical_lift_verified_exact =\n        (nS_hat == (0,1,0))`. `scalar_mult` is built from `jac_add`/`jac_dbl`,\n        the same routines used for every tabulated value. The supporting asserts\n        do not close the loop either: `curve_ok(S_hat, MOD)` tests only that the\n        lifted point satisfies the curve equation (membership, not the group\n        law); `assert valuation(f0, r, WORK_MOD) == 1` is itself evaluated\n        through `f_of_x0` -> `scalar_mult`. RUN-2's manifest records the field\n        `canonical_lift_S_hat.independent_certificate` whose own text reads\n        \"recomputed via the SAME double-and-add scalar multiplication used\n        everywhere else in this script\". The field name asserts an independence\n        the field body denies.\n      why_it_bites: >-\n        My Objection OBJ-6 shows the measured image is FORCED once the order-n\n        certificate holds. So the entire empirical content of both runs rests on\n        that one certificate -- and that certificate has no implementation\n        independence in either run, and shares 100% of its code across the two.\n        A wrong-but-self-consistent group law would satisfy it in both runs\n        identically. AGENTS.md \"Review architecture\" names exactly this failure\n        mode: \"recomputing from the producer's own artifacts reproduces a\n        wrong-but-self-consistent implementation faithfully, which is exactly\n        the failure mode validation cannot see.\"\n      unused_control_already_in_the_file: >-\n        Each script already contains a genuinely independent second\n        implementation of the group law -- the F_p affine routines `fp_add` /\n        `fp_mul`, used only during instance setup -- and NEITHER RUN EVER\n        CROSS-CHECKS the Z/p^r Jacobian arithmetic against it. Three lines\n        asserting `affine_x(scalar_mult(k, S_hat, MOD), MOD) % p ==\n        fp_mul(k, S, p, A)[0]` for k = 1..n-1 would exercise the shared core\n        against independent code at zero marginal cost. It is not there.\n      what_would_change_it: FG-1, FG-2.\n\n    - id: OBJ-3\n      title: >-\n        The null-lift control discriminates a shared MATHEMATICAL-PREMISE error\n        and a narrow band of input-invariant code errors. It does NOT\n        discriminate a shared CODE error, and it supplies no cross-run\n        independence at all because it is itself common-mode.","lineStart":120,"lineEnd":199,"totalLines":961,"truncated":true}},"title":"coordination/experiments/EXP-ECDLP-c373eb/batches/BATCH-e8a3a7/reviews/TASK-20260903-d02517/red-team-report.yaml","time":{"start":1789094198054,"end":1789094198113}},"id":"prt_08e52ed4e001daRgBLo8dqtQe5","sessionID":"ses_f72344e8bffef224SU4Jqwq9zP","messageID":"msg_08e51182100128pmyHf6im84mX"}}
